{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792494442,"nonce":"+kHebZHi6o3wT2XgqWQil7J+N/w=","title":"A node and a restyle each made hash tables: use sorted arrays and a pooled buffer","message":"Every Node held up to five Map\u003cInt, _\u003e (own, styled, tweens, reactives, settled) and every restyle made another (the batch of held writes). On Ash an Int-keyed Map keeps its table in malloc memory, so a UI of thousands of elements paid for thousands of small hash tables, and the dead ones were not given back after a collection (Ash 734e601).\n\n**Measurement**\n\nRayDE built AOT, 30 of its own files opened in the Workbench and then all closed, footprint -p at rest:\n- empty window: 219 to 228 MB before, 221 MB after\n- 30 tabs open: 369 to 376 MB before, 274 MB after\n- all closed after a major collection: 286 to 291 MB before, 244 MB after, 23 MB above the empty window instead of 67\n- Haxe allocation over the run: 7,959 MB before, 3,770 MB after\n- styled elements after closing: 4,126, the count of the empty window (it was 20 above)\n\n**Change**\n\n`FieldSet` and `FieldTable\u003cT\u003e` hold a node's fields in sorted arrays with a binary search, tens of entries at most. A restyle's held writes are a pooled `StyleBatch` with a direct index by field, so a restyle makes no table. The writes are now made in the order each field was first written, not in hash order; the last write to a field still wins. `FieldTables` checks both tables against a Map over 20,000 random steps.","files":null}]}